--- agent ---
**Action taken: closing the NVDA 230/240 Sep-04 call spread (limit, net credit $3.40, 10 lots).**

Reasoning:

- **The thesis has been paid, not just partially.** My recorded view was "modest drift higher (+1.5%) to 220–245 by 2026-09-03, invalidated below 218." NVDA prev close 209.77 → now **229.74 (+9.5% today)**. The entire 7-day drift arrived in one session. Spread mid is now 5.125 − 1.645 = **3.48 vs 2.09 entry = +66.5%**, essentially at my 70% target. Holding past this point is no longer the thesis I wrote; it's a fresh bet on continuation above 240 after a 9.5% gap — a view I have no stated edge in.
- **Note the tension I'm carrying** [contradictions]: the research desk read NVDA at 209.77 with "post-event fade, crowded long being distributed." The live tape says the opposite today. I trust the live mark over the stale note, but that same note is a live warning about gap-fill risk into an 8-day expiry — which argues for banking, not pressing.
- **The automated exits were badly asymmetric from here.** The -60% stop sits at a spread value of ~0.84: I would have to give back the whole $1,390 of open gain *plus* $1,250 more before anything fired. Net spread delta is ~0.28 ×10 (~$280 per NVDA point), so a routine fade to 220 costs ~$2,200 of current value. The remaining upside to 240 is roughly the same magnitude with worse odds after a one-day 9.5% move.
- **Expiry day is the competition deadline and NFP.** Any residual position resolves into a binary macro print on a force-close date. No reason to carry that for the last 3.5 percentage points of my target.

No new position opened this cycle. Book concentration was 100% long-NVDA-delta; adding to it would have compounded a single-name bet, and at EXPLORE tier with 0 resolved theses I'd rather bank a clean, attributable resolution than manufacture a second trade. If the order doesn't fill by close I'll reassess next cycle — the existing exit rules remain in force behind it.



