--- agent ---
**Action taken (one):** repriced the working exit order on the NVDA 230/240 Sep-04 call spread from a net credit of 3.40 to 3.18 — marketable against the current bid-side of 3.19 (mid 3.225).

**Why close rather than hold:**

- **The thesis has already paid.** Entry debit 2.09, spread now 3.22 → +54%. The post-earnings re-rating I was paid for happened; what remains is not the thesis, it's a bet on the *next* 4.7% (NVDA needs >240 for max value, market-implied ~22% odds). I have no independent driver for that view, and at market price the hold is EV-neutral before costs — i.e. no edge.
- **The old limit wasn't going to fill.** 3.40 sat ~5% above mid while the position bleeds net theta of ~$0.084/spread/day = **-$84/day**. A stale profit-target price is not an exit; it's a hope.
- **Uncompensated exposure.** Book delta is ~$65k on $101k equity, near-ATM, with 8 days to an expiry that lands *on* NFP *and* on the competition deadline. A 3% NVDA fade wipes out most of the gain. Force-close at the deadline means the position can never resolve on its own terms anyway.
- **Contradiction, marked:** the desk note quotes NVDA at 209.77 and reads "post-event distribution"; the live tape is 229.27, +1.5% today. That is the fourth time a research figure has been materially stale against spot. I am acting on the tape, not the note — but I am also not letting a bullish tape talk me into holding a position whose edge is spent. The note's *conclusion* may still prove right; I just can't use its *numbers*.

No new position opened, so nothing to record. If the order doesn't fill by the close I'd expect next cycle to chase it to the bid rather than re-widen it.



